# Study apparatus for the KJV Rightly Divided — mid-Acts / Acts 9 voice.
# INTROS: book -> {"t": intro html (teaches placement), "k": key reference}

INTROS = {
# ---------------- SECTION I: THE PROPHETIC PROGRAM ----------------
"Genesis": {"t":"The book of beginnings: creation, the fall, the flood, and the call of Abraham. Here God launches the prophetic program by promising a seed, a land, and a nation — the covenant line through which He will deal with Israel and, through Israel, the earth. Everything Israel later hopes for is planted in these promises.","k":"Genesis 12:1-3"},
"Exodus": {"t":"Israel is redeemed out of Egypt by blood and power, brought to Sinai, and given the Law. A people becomes a covenant nation under Moses. This is national, earthly, and conditional — the very order the mystery revealed to Paul does not operate under.","k":"Exodus 19:5-6"},
"Leviticus": {"t":"The priesthood, the sacrifices, and the holiness code by which Israel could approach God. It is Israel's manual of worship under the old covenant — a system of types pointing forward, wholly within the prophetic program.","k":"Leviticus 17:11"},
"Numbers": {"t":"Israel's wilderness journey and the unbelief that kept a generation from the land. The record of a nation being proved and disciplined under the Law.","k":"Numbers 14:22-23"},
"Deuteronomy": {"t":"Moses restates the Law to the new generation before they enter the land, setting before Israel the blessing and the curse. The covenant terms of Israel's earthly program are laid out plainly.","k":"Deuteronomy 30:15-19"},
"Joshua": {"t":"The conquest and division of the promised land — God keeping His land-promise to Israel. The kingdom program advances geographically as Israel takes possession.","k":"Joshua 21:45"},
"Judges": {"t":"A cycle of apostasy, oppression, and deliverance in the days when Israel had no king. It shows the nation's need for the righteous King the prophets will promise.","k":"Judges 21:25"},
"Ruth": {"t":"A kinsman-redeemer restores an empty widow and preserves the royal line leading to David. A quiet chapter in the prophetic program guarding the lineage of Israel's coming King.","k":"Ruth 4:13-17"},
"1 Samuel": {"t":"Israel moves from judges to monarchy; Saul is raised and rejected, David is anointed. The kingdom takes visible form as God prepares the man after His own heart.","k":"1 Samuel 8:6-7"},
"2 Samuel": {"t":"David's reign, and the covenant that guarantees an everlasting throne to his house. The Davidic covenant is the backbone of Israel's kingdom hope and the prophets' expectation.","k":"2 Samuel 7:12-16"},
"1 Kings": {"t":"Solomon, the temple, and then the kingdom torn in two. The high point and fracture of Israel's monarchy, all within the prophetic line.","k":"1 Kings 9:3-5"},
"2 Kings": {"t":"The decline of the divided kingdom to captivity — Israel to Assyria, Judah to Babylon. The prophetic program bends under the nation's covenant-breaking.","k":"2 Kings 17:22-23"},
"1 Chronicles": {"t":"David's reign retold from a priestly, temple-centered view, tracing the covenant line. A restatement of Israel's royal and worship history.","k":"1 Chronicles 17:11-14"},
"2 Chronicles": {"t":"Judah's kings and the temple, from Solomon's glory to the exile. The prophetic program measured by faithfulness to the house of God.","k":"2 Chronicles 7:14"},
"Ezra": {"t":"The return from Babylon and the rebuilding of the temple — God restoring a remnant to the land according to prophecy. Israel's program resumes after judgment.","k":"Ezra 1:1-3"},
"Nehemiah": {"t":"Jerusalem's walls rebuilt and the covenant renewed. The restored remnant is re-established under the Law in the land.","k":"Nehemiah 8:8"},
"Esther": {"t":"God's hidden hand preserving the Jews from destruction in dispersion. Even scattered, Israel is kept for the sake of the promises.","k":"Esther 4:14"},
"Job": {"t":"The oldest wrestling with suffering and the sovereignty of God. Wisdom literature within Israel's Scriptures, framing a hope of a living Redeemer.","k":"Job 19:25-26"},
"Psalms": {"t":"Israel's hymnbook of praise, lament, and prophecy — rich with the Messiah and His kingdom. These songs breathe the earthly, prophetic hope of the nation.","k":"Psalm 2:6-8"},
"Proverbs": {"t":"Practical wisdom for living in the fear of the LORD under the covenant. Instruction for God's earthly people.","k":"Proverbs 1:7"},
"Ecclesiastes": {"t":"Life examined “under the sun” apart from the fuller revelation later given. It ends by driving man back to fear God and keep His commandments.","k":"Ecclesiastes 12:13"},
"Song of Solomon": {"t":"A poem of covenant love, long read as picturing the LORD and Israel. It belongs to the affections of the prophetic people.","k":"Song of Solomon 2:16"},
"Isaiah": {"t":"The great prophet of both judgment and salvation, foretelling the suffering Servant and the reigning King. Israel's kingdom hope is drawn in its fullest colors here.","k":"Isaiah 9:6-7"},
"Jeremiah": {"t":"Judgment before the exile, and the promise of a new covenant made — note carefully — with the house of Israel and Judah. A prophetic-program covenant, not the mystery.","k":"Jeremiah 31:31-34"},
"Lamentations": {"t":"Tears over fallen Jerusalem, and mercy remembered in the midst of ruin. The grief of the prophetic people under discipline.","k":"Lamentations 3:22-23"},
"Ezekiel": {"t":"Visions of judgment, then of a restored nation, a new heart, and a millennial temple. The earthly kingdom hope is portrayed in vivid detail.","k":"Ezekiel 37:24-28"},
"Daniel": {"t":"The times of the Gentiles and the everlasting kingdom that shall break every earthly empire. Prophecy's timeline for Israel and the nations.","k":"Daniel 2:44"},
"Hosea": {"t":"Israel's unfaithfulness and God's relentless love that will yet restore her. A prophetic picture of the nation's fall and future return.","k":"Hosea 3:4-5"},
"Joel": {"t":"The day of the LORD and the promised outpouring of the Spirit on Israel — the passage Peter cites at Pentecost, squarely within the prophetic program.","k":"Joel 2:28-32"},
"Amos": {"t":"Judgment on injustice, closing with the promise to raise up the fallen tabernacle of David. Kingdom restoration for Israel.","k":"Amos 9:11"},
"Obadiah": {"t":"The doom of Edom and the day of the LORD upon the nations. A short prophetic word of judgment and Israel's deliverance.","k":"Obadiah 1:15"},
"Jonah": {"t":"Mercy extended to Nineveh, and a reluctant prophet taught God's compassion. Gentile mercy shown within the bounds of prophecy.","k":"Jonah 4:11"},
"Micah": {"t":"Judgment and hope, naming Bethlehem as the birthplace of Israel's Ruler. The Messiah of the prophetic program is foretold.","k":"Micah 5:2"},
"Nahum": {"t":"The fall of Nineveh — God's justice on a once-spared city. A prophetic word of comfort to Judah.","k":"Nahum 1:7"},
"Habakkuk": {"t":"The prophet's questions answered: the just shall live by his faith. A verse Paul later takes up, yet spoken first within Israel's prophetic hope.","k":"Habakkuk 2:4"},
"Zephaniah": {"t":"The day of the LORD in judgment, and a restored, rejoicing remnant. Israel's future gathered out of wrath.","k":"Zephaniah 3:17"},
"Haggai": {"t":"A call to rebuild the temple, with the promise of a greater glory to come. The prophetic program pressing the remnant to God's house.","k":"Haggai 2:9"},
"Zechariah": {"t":"Visions of the coming King — riding lowly, then reigning — and Jerusalem's day of the LORD. Kingdom prophecy in concentrated form.","k":"Zechariah 9:9"},
"Malachi": {"t":"The last Old Testament voice, promising the messenger and the coming day. It closes the prophetic program awaiting Messiah, with no hint yet of the mystery.","k":"Malachi 3:1"},
"Matthew": {"t":"Christ presented as Israel's promised King, offering the kingdom to the nation. Remember Romans 15:8 — He came “a minister of the circumcision”; His earthly ministry served the prophetic program, not yet the mystery.","k":"Matthew 15:24"},
"Mark": {"t":"The Servant-King in action, preaching the gospel of the kingdom to Israel. A Gospel of the prophetic program, moving swiftly from work to work.","k":"Mark 1:14-15"},
"Luke": {"t":"The Son of man among Israel, with the compassion that will reach the Gentiles — yet still under the kingdom offer to the nation. Prophetic-program ground, written with a Gentile reader in view.","k":"Luke 19:9-10"},
"John": {"t":"Written that you might believe Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God. Its “believe” gospel is precious, but its setting is Christ's earthly ministry to Israel, before the mystery was made known.","k":"John 20:31"},
# ---------------- SECTION II: THE TRANSITION ----------------
"Acts": {"t":"The hinge of the Bible. It opens with Pentecost and the twelve still offering the kingdom to Israel — the prophetic program running on. Then at chapter 9 the risen Lord strikes down Saul and raises up Paul: the Body of Christ begins, and a new dispensation of grace is committed to him. Across Acts, Israel is progressively set aside in unbelief while Paul's ministry to the Gentiles rises. Read it as a transition, not a template.","k":"Acts 9 & 28:28"},
# ---------------- SECTION III: THE MYSTERY (PAUL'S 13) ----------------
"Romans": {"t":"The foundational doctrine of the Body of Christ: all under sin, justified freely by grace through faith in Christ's blood. Chapters 9–11 explain Israel's present setting-aside, and the close names outright the mystery kept secret since the world began. Begin your doctrine here.","k":"Romans 3:24-28; 16:25"},
"1 Corinthians": {"t":"Correction and order for a carnal assembly — the one body baptized by one Spirit, and the resurrection gospel stated plainly: Christ died for our sins, was buried, and rose again. Grace working out in the local church of this dispensation.","k":"1 Corinthians 15:1-4"},
"2 Corinthians": {"t":"Paul defends his apostleship and unfolds the ministry of reconciliation — the ministry of the Spirit surpassing the ministry of the letter engraven in stones. The grace-ministry of the mystery over against the old covenant.","k":"2 Corinthians 5:17-21"},
"Galatians": {"t":"The charter of grace against law: a man is justified by the faith of Christ and not by the works of the law. Paul insists his gospel came not from man but by the revelation of Jesus Christ — the mystery's independent origin.","k":"Galatians 2:16; 1:11-12"},
"Ephesians": {"t":"The summit of the mystery: Jew and Gentile made one new man, the Body of Christ, blessed and seated together in the heavenly places. Here the secret hid in God is unveiled most fully — the distinctive hope and calling of this dispensation.","k":"Ephesians 2:5-6; 3:3-9"},
"Philippians": {"t":"Joy and the mind of Christ, pressing toward the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus. The heavenly citizenship of the Body shapes the believer's walk.","k":"Philippians 3:14, 20"},
"Colossians": {"t":"The preeminent Christ, in whom dwells all fulness, and the mystery now made manifest: Christ in you, the hope of glory. Complete in Him, the believer is freed from ordinances and shadow.","k":"Colossians 1:24-27"},
"1 Thessalonians": {"t":"The blessed hope stated plainly — the Lord Himself descending, and the dead in Christ and the living caught up together to meet Him in the air. This is the hope of the Body, distinct from Israel's earthly kingdom.","k":"1 Thessalonians 4:16-18"},
"2 Thessalonians": {"t":"Comfort under persecution and correction about the day of the LORD, which had not come. Paul distinguishes the Body's gathering to Christ from the prophesied day of wrath upon the world.","k":"2 Thessalonians 2:1-3"},
"1 Timothy": {"t":"Order and sound doctrine for the house of God, the church of the living God. Pastoral instruction for conduct in this present dispensation of grace.","k":"1 Timothy 3:15-16"},
"2 Timothy": {"t":"Paul's last words, and the pattern-verse of this whole edition: study to rightly divide the word of truth. A charge to guard sound doctrine and endure as the age darkens — marching orders for the Body of Christ.","k":"2 Timothy 2:15"},
"Titus": {"t":"Sound doctrine issuing in good works, and the grace of God that teaches us to live looking for the blessed hope. Grace instructs; it does not license.","k":"Titus 2:11-13"},
"Philemon": {"t":"Grace made personal: a runaway slave received back as a beloved brother, his debt charged to Paul's account. A living picture of imputation — the gospel of the Body in miniature.","k":"Philemon 1:17-18"},
# ---------------- SECTION IV: PROPHETIC EPISTLES & CONSUMMATION ----------------
"Hebrews": {"t":"Written to Hebrew believers to show Christ's superiority to the old covenant order — better priest, better sacrifice, better covenant. It is not one of Paul's thirteen and is not addressed to the Body of Christ; it belongs to the prophetic program, ministering to Israel in transition. Placed here, with the Jewish epistles, on purpose.","k":"Hebrews 8:6-13"},
"James": {"t":"Addressed to the twelve tribes scattered abroad — a kingdom epistle in which faith is proved by works. Its audience and emphasis are Israel's, not the mystery's.","k":"James 1:1; 2:14-26"},
"1 Peter": {"t":"To the strangers of the dispersion, a chosen generation and royal priesthood — language of Israel's kingdom hope under suffering. Peter writes as apostle to the circumcision.","k":"1 Peter 1:1; 2:9"},
"2 Peter": {"t":"Warning of false teachers and the certainty of the day of the LORD, in which the heavens pass away with a great noise. Prophetic-program expectation to the end.","k":"2 Peter 3:10"},
"1 John": {"t":"Fellowship, love, and the assurance of eternal life, with tests to discern truth from error. A pastoral letter to believers in the Jewish-Christian sphere.","k":"1 John 5:13"},
"2 John": {"t":"A short charge to walk in truth and to refuse the deceiver who denies Christ come in the flesh. Guarding sound testimony.","k":"2 John 1:9-10"},
"3 John": {"t":"Commendation of hospitality to faithful workers and rebuke of a domineering man. A brief word on faithfulness in the assembly.","k":"3 John 1:11"},
"Jude": {"t":"An urgent call to contend earnestly for the faith against apostates, sealed with warnings of judgment. It looks toward the Lord's coming in judgment upon the ungodly.","k":"Jude 1:3"},
"Revelation": {"t":"The unveiling of Jesus Christ: the day of the LORD, the judgment of the nations, and the kingdom brought at last to earth. The prophetic program reaches its consummation — the very hope the Old Testament prophets foretold, now fulfilled.","k":"Revelation 1:1; 19-22"},
}

# ---------------- FRONT MATTER: HOW TO READ ----------------
HOWTO_TITLE = "How to Read This Bible"
HOWTO = [
 ("Two programs, one God",
  "All Scripture is given by inspiration of God and is profitable (2 Timothy 3:16), but not all Scripture is written <em>to</em> or <em>about</em> the same people. The Bible unfolds two distinct programs: God’s dealings with <strong>Israel</strong>, an earthly, prophetic, kingdom program foretold “since the world began” (Acts 3:21); and the <strong>Body of Christ</strong>, a heavenly program kept secret in God and revealed through the apostle Paul — the <strong>mystery</strong> (Ephesians 3:1-9)."),
 ("Where the change happens — Acts 9",
  "The turning point is not Genesis to Matthew, and not the cross alone. It is <strong>Acts chapter 9</strong>, where the risen Lord strikes down Saul of Tarsus and raises him up as the apostle of the Gentiles. There the Body of Christ begins and the dispensation of grace is committed to Paul. This edition marks that pivot inside the book of Acts. (This is the mid-Acts position — not Acts 13, and not Bullinger’s Acts 28.)"),
 ("Why Paul’s epistles come as a distinct section",
  "Paul was given a gospel by direct revelation (Galatians 1:11-12) and calls it repeatedly “my gospel” and “our gospel.” His thirteen epistles — Romans through Philemon — are the doctrine, position, and walk of the Body of Christ today. That is why they are gathered as their own section, the heart of this Bible."),
 ("How to use the rest",
  "The Old Testament, the Gospels, Acts, and the Hebrew epistles (Hebrews through Jude) with Revelation are all God’s Word and full of profit — but they are read as history, as prophecy, and as the program of Israel, rightly divided as to <em>application</em>. We learn from them; we do not build the doctrine of the Body of Christ upon them."),
 ("A word on Hebrews",
  "Hebrews is placed with the prophetic epistles, not with Paul’s thirteen. It is written to <em>the Hebrews</em>, showing Christ’s superiority to the old covenant order, and ministers to Israel. Whatever one concludes about its authorship, its audience and subject are not the mystery."),
]

